1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,585
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,585 --> 00:00:04,872
Episode 10.

3
00:00:04,872 --> 00:00:07,443
The Nighttime Sound Map.

4
00:00:07,443 --> 00:00:10,468
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:10,468 --> 00:00:15,596
What information returns to a bat after its call reaches an object?

6
00:00:15,596 --> 00:00:17,187
Ready?

7
00:00:17,187 --> 00:00:19,389
Let us find out.

8
00:00:19,389 --> 00:00:23,650
At sunset, a nature guide takes the club to a park.

9
00:00:23,650 --> 00:00:27,443
Everyone stays on the path and watches the sky.

10
00:00:27,443 --> 00:00:30,752
A small shape flies above the trees.

11
00:00:30,752 --> 00:00:33,224
Then another appears.

12
00:00:33,224 --> 00:00:35,340
Are those birds?

13
00:00:35,340 --> 00:00:37,585
Maya asks.

14
00:00:37,585 --> 00:00:40,582
They are bats, says the guide.

15
00:00:40,582 --> 00:00:44,232
Bats are mammals, and they can truly fly.

16
00:00:44,232 --> 00:00:47,599
Leo has heard that bats cannot see.

17
00:00:47,599 --> 00:00:51,789
The guide explains that bats have eyes and are not blind.

18
00:00:51,789 --> 00:00:56,136
Many also use sound to find their way and catch insects.

19
00:00:56,136 --> 00:00:59,303
She turns on a special detector.

20
00:00:59,303 --> 00:01:03,522
It changes some bat calls into sounds people can hear.

21
00:01:03,522 --> 00:01:06,392
The speaker makes quick clicks.

22
00:01:06,392 --> 00:01:08,863
Pip listens carefully.

23
00:01:08,863 --> 00:01:13,068
That sounds like someone typing a very urgent message.

24
00:01:13,068 --> 00:01:16,718
A bat sends out calls, the guide says.

25
00:01:16,718 --> 00:01:21,860
Sound bounces off objects, and returning echoes give it information.

26
00:01:21,860 --> 00:01:24,971
Like shouting in a large empty hall?

27
00:01:24,971 --> 00:01:27,187
Leo asks.

28
00:01:27,187 --> 00:01:32,826
The idea of an echo is similar, though a bat uses much finer information.

29
00:01:32,826 --> 00:01:37,059
Maya imagines a picture made from returning sounds.

30
00:01:37,059 --> 00:01:41,278
The bat turns quickly, then disappears over the pond.

31
00:01:41,278 --> 00:01:45,042
Could I find my missing sock by shouting at it?

32
00:01:45,042 --> 00:01:47,258
Leo asks.

33
00:01:47,258 --> 00:01:51,477
You could begin by looking under your bed, says Maya.

34
00:01:51,477 --> 00:01:56,264
Pip records a message for the sock: Please report your location.

35
00:01:56,264 --> 00:01:58,678
There is no reply.

36
00:01:58,678 --> 00:02:03,068
Some mysteries still need a flashlight and a careful search.

37
00:02:03,068 --> 00:02:06,221
Now, a little more to discover.

38
00:02:06,221 --> 00:02:11,988
Many bats use echolocation: they produce calls and listen to returning echoes.

39
00:02:11,988 --> 00:02:16,022
Those echoes can help them locate objects and prey.

40
00:02:16,022 --> 00:02:20,184
Many of the calls are too high-pitched for human ears.

41
00:02:20,184 --> 00:02:26,576
Bats also have vision, and different species rely on different senses to different degrees.

42
00:02:26,576 --> 00:02:29,744
Let us hear the main idea again.

43
00:02:29,744 --> 00:02:35,284
The club watched bats and heard a detector turn high calls into audible clicks.

44
00:02:35,284 --> 00:02:39,062
Returning echoes help many bats navigate.

45
00:02:39,062 --> 00:02:43,920
The guide also corrected a common mistake: bats are not blind.

46
00:02:43,920 --> 00:02:47,059
Here are three useful expressions.

47
00:02:47,059 --> 00:02:50,241
You can listen, or say them with me.

48
00:02:50,241 --> 00:02:53,196
Listen carefully to the sound.

49
00:02:53,196 --> 00:02:56,150
Listen carefully to the sound.

50
00:02:56,150 --> 00:02:58,380
What does it tell us?

51
00:02:58,380 --> 00:03:00,610
What does it tell us?

52
00:03:00,610 --> 00:03:03,409
It helps them find their way.

53
00:03:03,409 --> 00:03:06,207
It helps them find their way.

54
00:03:06,207 --> 00:03:09,204
Remember our listening question?

55
00:03:09,204 --> 00:03:14,332
What information returns to a bat after its call reaches an object?

56
00:03:14,332 --> 00:03:16,619
Here is the answer.

57
00:03:16,619 --> 00:03:19,090
An echo returns.

58
00:03:19,090 --> 00:03:23,920
The bat can use that returning sound to learn about what is nearby.

59
00:03:23,920 --> 00:03:27,883
And here is a question just for your imagination.

60
00:03:27,883 --> 00:03:33,210
Where have you heard an echo, and what made it different from the original sound?

61
00:03:33,210 --> 00:03:36,519
There is more than one possible answer.

62
00:03:36,519 --> 00:03:39,815
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
